W3 2026-11-25 06:46Z. Plain pinned build /tmp/ccx2shell-plain/ccextractor,
LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/ccx2shell-deps/root/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu,
script real-build/gdb-heap-pointers.cmd, breakpoint ccx_gxf.c:1220
(after parse_mpeg_packet). For each input the script reads 65536 bytes at
buffer+BUFSIZE (BUFSIZE=2097272), scans every aligned 8-byte word whose value
looks like a code/data address, and asks GDB `info symbol` to resolve it.
Observed output (verbatim filtered lines):

=== safe.gxf ===
Breakpoint 1, parse_media (demux=0x7fffe63ef010, len=4096, data=0x555556392cf0) at /tmp/ccx2shell-upstream-asan/src/lib_ccx/ccx_gxf.c:1220
HEAPSCAN buffer=55555641c990 usable=2097272 boundary=55555661ca08
HEAPSCAN codeptr_hits=0
=== attack.gxf ===
Breakpoint 1, parse_media (demux=0x7fffe63ef010, len=2101248, data=0x555556392cf0) at /tmp/ccx2shell-upstream-asan/src/lib_ccx/ccx_gxf.c:1220
HEAPSCAN buffer=55555641c990 usable=2097272 boundary=55555661ca08
HEAPSCAN codeptr_hits=0
=== readback.gxf ===
Breakpoint 1, parse_media (demux=0x7fffe63ef010, len=2101248, data=0x555556392cf0) at /tmp/ccx2shell-upstream-asan/src/lib_ccx/ccx_gxf.c:1220
HEAPSCAN buffer=55555641c990 usable=0 boundary=55555661ca08
HEAPSCAN codeptr_hits=0

Untouched next-chunk header on safe.gxf (same breakpoint, x/4gx
data->buffer+2097272):
0x55555661ca08:	0x0000000000020601	0x0000000000000000
0x55555661ca18:	0x0000000000000000	0x0000000000000000

Interpretation and limits: the safe-input scan is the meaningful control —
in this build/heap layout the 64 KiB after the 2097272-byte allocation
contains no resolvable code pointer, so the 3976-byte overflow reaches no
function pointer to overwrite directly. The attack/readback scans are taken
after our own payload has filled that region and are not independent
evidence of the pre-write layout. `malloc_usable_size` returned 0 for
readback.gxf, consistent with corrupted malloc metadata at that point; that
value is not used as a bound in the scan. `info symbol` resolves symbols in
the loaded main binary and shared libraries; an opaque or unloaded code
address would not be counted. This is one build and one heap layout, not a
proof for all layouts. No rung claim rests on this negative.
